  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 205 AM CDT Mon Aug 31 2026\n\nOn Wednesday, upper level ridging is expected to amplify across the \nregion, which will help usher in a warmer, drier pattern. Guidance \nremains in strong agreement on this ridging building by mid week \nlasting into next week. A look at GEFS spaghetti plots builds fairly \nhigh confidence in this pattern, as even though the solutions begin \nto diverge by Friday, all of the solutions bring upper level ridging \nsomewhere across the Northern Plains. Cluster analysis also remains \nin strong agreement, highlighting upper level ridging remaining \nthrough next week. This will help keep warmer than average \ntemperatures across the region into next week. For reference, our \ntypical highs are around 85 degrees, becoming 84 by the weekend. \nCurrent forecast highs are mostly in the upper 80s to mid 90s, \nthough some portions of southwest Nebraska may see highs push into \nthe upper 90s before the end of the week. These highs are consistent \nwith the 75th to 90th percentile high temperatures compared to our \nlocal climatology. \n\nThe stronger upper level ridging will also help keep precipitation \nchances fairly limited Wednesday onward. LAtest NBM guidance does \nstill keep some slight chance PoPs, generally less than 20 percent, \neach night this weekend. However, based on ensemble guidance, any \nprecipitation chances will be for fairly light precipitation. Latest \nensemble guidance continues to show a less than 20 percent chance of \nprecipitation exceeding one tenth of an inch late week into the\nweekend. Better chances, generally 30 to 40 percent, are seen \nfor one hundredth of an inch or greater. Deterministic guidance \ndoes suggest potential for some weaker shortwaves to track \nthrough the main upper level flow, which could support some very\nlight precipitation, and tend to think this is what the NBM is \nkeying on for potential precipitation. Still, if anything were \nto fall, based on current guidance, would expect it to be very \nlight.",
